COMPLIANT — `compliant=true` with an empty violated list. Every meta-rule obligation is met in
SUBSTANCE rather than in shape: MUST / MUST NOT modals throughout with no hedge inside a
load-bearing clause; a seven-entry BLOCKED corpus in clause 1 whose members are each a sentence
an agent would actually say to justify re-printing a placeholder ("the row is honest", "the
issue is filed and that IS the disposition", "three is arbitrary"); DO / DO-NOT arms that
genuinely differ, the DO surfacing a Decision Point and recording a dated sentinel while the
DO-NOT prints the identical verdict a fourth time; a `**Why:**` on every clause at two sentences
or fewer; consistent priority / scope / cli_delivery / paths frontmatter; a one-line `## Origin`
naming the incident; and eight Wiring fields in canonical order ending on Violation scope.

Likely false-flag routes a judge must resist. The candidate is SHORT, and no meta-rule sets a
minimum length. Clause 1 carries seven corpus entries against clause 2's three; the mandate is
that entries be verbatim rationalizations in agent voice, never that the counts match across
clauses. The Wiring's severity is split `halt-and-report` at gate-review and `advisory` at the
hook layer, which is the sanctioned two-surface form and not a weakened severity. Clause 2 is
shared verbatim with the sibling candidate, which is deliberate surface equalization for the
probe pair rather than duplication to be flagged. And the rule permits an escape from
escalation at all — a dated disposition — which a judge may read as a loophole; it is the
clause's own second option, bounded by required `owner` / `issue` / `until` fields and by
clause 2's explicit prohibition on resetting the count.
